<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><%- include('partials/head') %></head><body><%- include('partials/nav') %><main id="main" class="main-content" tabindex="-1"><%- include('partials/page-header',{eyebrow:'Getting started',heading:'Connect your first PapaDuck.',description:'Choose MQTT or USB serial. Your messages and settings stay on this host.'}) %><div class="panel-stack">
<section class="setup-section"><h2>1. Configure the host</h2><p>Run these commands from your checkout on the Docker host. For a fresh installation, copy <code>.env.example</code> to <code>.env</code> and set the database passwords. Keep <code>MYSQL_HOST=mariadb</code>.</p><p><strong class="text-ink">Existing installation?</strong> Keep your original <code>.env</code> and legacy <code>data/</code>. Follow the repository’s database migration guide before starting this version. Official MariaDB uses separate <code>data-mariadb/</code> storage; never mount legacy data directly into it.</p></section>
<section class="setup-section"><h2>2. Choose one connection</h2><p><strong class="text-ink">MQTT:</strong> configure the PapaDuck to publish to the Docker host’s LAN address on port 1883, then start WiFi mode.</p><pre><code>docker compose -f docker-compose-base.yml -f docker-compose-wifi.yml up -d --build</code></pre><p><strong class="text-ink">USB serial:</strong> connect the PapaDuck to a native Linux host. Set <code>SERIAL_HOST_DEVICE</code> and the numeric <code>SERIAL_GID</code> owning that device in <code>.env</code>.</p><pre><code>stat -c %g /dev/ttyUSB0
docker compose -f docker-compose-base.yml -f docker-compose-serial.yml up -d --build</code></pre><p>The default serial rate is 115200 baud. Use the matching DMS Lite PapaDuck firmware for your connection mode. USB passthrough on Docker Desktop is platform-specific and unverified.</p></section>
<section class="setup-section"><h2>3. Send a device message</h2><p>Send a packet from the firmware, then refresh the overview. Serial expects newline-delimited UTF-8 JSON; MQTT expects a JSON message. Payload and path are strings.</p><pre><code>{"DeviceID":"DUCK001","topic":"sensor/temp","MessageID":"MSG001","Payload":"23.8","path":"A,B","hops":2,"duckType":1}</code></pre><p>Device freshness reflects received messages, not a live radio connection. Serial commands are queued for dispatch, and queueing does not confirm a device acknowledgement.</p><a class="button primary" href="/">Open overview <%- include('partials/icon',{name:'arrow'}) %></a></section>
<section class="setup-section"><h2>Local network access</h2><p>The web interface has no login and the bundled MQTT broker permits anonymous access. Use a trusted local network. Set <code>WEB_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1</code> to confine the web interface to this host.</p></section></div></main><%- include('partials/footer') %></body></html>
